<p>We've worked on enough <a href="/recent-projects/">Hartselle homes</a> to know that downspouts are often overlooked. Older homes downtown frequently have original downspouts undersized by modern standards. Newer construction often uses builder-grade downspouts that meet minimum code but can't handle heavy storms.</p>

<p>Hartselle's mix of town properties and more rural acreage creates varied drainage challenges. In-town lots have specific grading patterns. Properties outside town often have different soil compositions and drainage needs related to the agricultural land throughout Morgan County.</p>

<p>When downspouts can't handle the volume, gutters back up and overflow. When downspouts dump water too close to the foundation, that water pools and causes damage. Both problems are common around here — and both are fixable.</p>

<p><strong>Our opinion:</strong> Before you spend $2,000+ on new gutters because of overflow, let us look at your downspouts. Half the overflow problems we see in Hartselle are downspout issues — not enough of them, wrong size, bad positioning. That's a much cheaper fix than replacing the entire system.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>A good downspout system has three components: enough capacity, proper positioning, and effective water dispersal.</p>

<p><strong>Capacity:</strong> One downspout for every 35-40 linear feet of gutter run, maximum. And they should be 3x4 inch, not 2x3. The larger size handles roughly twice the flow rate. During intense storms, that difference determines whether water drains away or backs up and overflows next to your foundation.</p>

<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> Downspouts go where water naturally collects — typically corners and low points in gutter runs. Bad positioning creates dead zones where water pools instead of draining.</p>

<p><strong>Dispersal:</strong> Water needs to exit far from your foundation. Extensions should reach 6-10 feet minimum. For Morgan County's clay soil, underground drains routing water 20+ feet away provide the best protection.</p>

<p><strong>Warning from experience:</strong> Those corrugated black flex pipes people bury in their yards? They clog within 2-3 years. Every time. The corrugations trap debris, roots grow into them, and water stops flowing. If you want buried drainage, use smooth PVC. We dig up failed flex pipe installations all over Hartselle.</p>
{% endset %}

